{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/frontend.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:09:45.486898Z","id":"501ebc24ff86130ffefc506897ad8e537d128850eb5543dbd0fd76f76c8adb7a","body":{"detail":"Failure: `pub fn top_bit(s: u32) -> bool { ((1 << s) >> 31) == 1 }` is accepted by `provium compile` and translated to `(.binary .eq (.binary .shr (.binary .shl (.uint 32 1) (.var 0)) (.uint 32 31)) (.uint 32 1))` (u32 arithmetic). rustc infers the unconstrained literal `1` as i32 (shift RHS type does not constrain the LHS; see https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/operator-expr.html#arithmetic-and-logical-binary-operators and integer fallback in https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/expressions/literal-expr.html#integer-literal-expressions), so `1 << 31` is i32::MIN and `>> 31` is an arithmetic shift giving -1: native `top_bit(31)` returns false (checked with rustc -C overflow-checks=yes), while the generated Lean returns true. The rustc type check in project.rs does not catch this because the source is valid Rust; only Provium's own inference diverges. `Compiler::expr` computes the operand type as `hint(left).or_else(|| hint(right))` for every binary operator, and `hint` (lines 269-279) does the same for nested binaries, which is wrong for Shl/Shr whose right operand is independent of the result type. The same rule also wrongly rejects valid code such as `let m: u32 = 1 << s` with `s: u8`.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":397},"end":{"line":402},"content_hash":"c87a35d3f55677be61a6e964dbfd6eb68dbbe1d9f4548b572062fcbf10a906b6"},"suggested_fix":"For Op::Shl/Op::Shr never derive the left operand type from the right operand: use hint(left).or(expected) for the left and type the right operand independently (or keep requiring the same type but reject when the left type is only inferable from the right). In `hint`, return hint(left) only for shift operators. Add a regression fixture with `((1 << s) >> 31) == 1`.","summary":"Scalar frontend types an unsuffixed shift LHS from the shift-count operand, so `1 << s` gets the count's type where rustc defaults to i32","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/extract.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:09:45.486984Z","id":"1d4067505bc5499dfabbf9dc8a43348720e0cabb8ef774add40c57d270ff9ffc","body":{"detail":"Failure: with `fn clamp(x: u64) -> u64 { if x > 10 { 10 } else { x } }` and `impl S { pub fn get(&self) -> u64 { clamp(self.a) } pub fn other(&self) -> u64 { self.a + 1 } }`, a project with slices `{method: S::get, name: get_value, select: [tail]}` and `{method: S::other, name: clamp, select: [tail]}` compiles successfully: Source.rs becomes `fn get_value(a: u64) -> u64 { clamp(a) } fn clamp(x: u64) -> u64 { x + 1 }` and `get_value_ir` is `a + 1`, whereas the Rust `S::get` returns `min(a, 10)`-like clamping. `extract` rejects patterns and macros in a slice but not call expressions; project.rs joins all abstracted slices into one source file, and frontend.rs resolves `clamp(...)` against that file's functions, which are named by the user-chosen `slice.name`, not by the original crate item. rustc type-checks only the abstracted file, so it cannot notice the substitution. README (\"Bindings are explicit abstraction assumptions\") does not cover silently rebinding calls.","kind":"blocker","span":{"start":{"line":169},"end":{"line":343},"content_hash":"70734be318527853922d482ea78d666f661bdcac5935317f2c6c3e187432c0b1"},"suggested_fix":"Reject `Expr::Call` (and any path expression that is not an abstraction binding) inside a selected slice, or resolve each call to the original crate item and require the referenced function to be translated in full under its original name. At minimum, reject slice names that collide with any item name in the source file and reject calls whose callee is another slice.","summary":"Expression slices resolve free-function calls to other slices by slice name, so a call to a crate function can be captured by an unrelated slice","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/extract.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:09:45.487020Z","id":"37446de3c233e24e49f3cc085322b4658f373feab7b81080883fc5d24468da9e","body":{"detail":"`navigate` collects every `let` whose identifier is in `parts[1..]` and accepts when `matches.len() == parts.len() - 1`. For a block with `let x = e; let x = e;` and selector `let:x:y`, two `x` initializers match and `y` (absent) is never checked, yet evidence records the selection as covering `x` and `y`. Selected-occurrence equality limits the semantic impact, but the evidence's claim of which bindings were selected is wrong and the README says selectors must match all expected occurrences.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":64},"end":{"line":86},"content_hash":"627064a80e2c82eb1588b0fb904075a902661dfaf34d24279c550a3c156d38cf"},"suggested_fix":"Require exactly one `let` per requested name (and reject shadowed duplicates), returning initializers in the selector's order.","summary":"The `let:a:b` selector counts matches instead of checking each named binding, so a missing name is masked by a shadowed one","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:09:45.487106Z","id":"eb32c17fd2f8822089b28d8b0b830faf520450993d396a7dd1bed5fe4f2a09c2","body":{"detail":"In `Crate::file`, only modules, structs, impls and methods have their attributes checked when `self.cfg` is None. `Item::Use` (lines 274-338) records imports regardless of `#[cfg(...)]`, `Item::Enum` (349-358) stores enums and their variants without inspecting item or variant attributes, `Item::ExternCrate` and free functions fall into `_ => {}`. `Crate::load` is public and is used by the `scalar_method` project path (project.rs `compile`), and its rustc check passes no cfg beyond defaults. A cfg-disabled `use crate::a::Vec;` therefore resolves a field type to a crate struct while rustc resolves `Vec` from the prelude, and an enum variant behind `#[cfg(feature = \"x\")]` is treated as present by backends that enumerate variants. README states that unsupported conditional attributes reject verification.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":245},"end":{"line":491},"content_hash":"7537bad6438526a1ebf35d364676678c83f1d60269b065e6508d18593ab8aae0"},"suggested_fix":"When no Configuration is supplied, reject any non-doc/non-allowlisted attribute on every item kind (use, enum and its variants, trait, extern crate, const, fn) as the struct and impl arms already do, or make the scalar_method path use `load_configured` with rustc's printed cfg like `methods::verify`.","summary":"Unconfigured `Crate::load` silently admits cfg-attributed imports, enums, traits and extern crates instead of rejecting them","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:09:45.487163Z","id":"a9275725a5bf3b92bebc7458a3320287d8ca5c9e463285d1de8b150184e921e5","body":{"detail":"The loader rejects a fixed list of shadowed names (Option, bool, u8.., Result, Ok, Err, Some) in four separate places (lines 280-289, 339-348, 381-389, 430-441) plus generic-parameter checks at 685-691 and 733-739 with a shorter list (no Result/Ok/Err/Some). `core`/`std`/`Default` shadowing (`mod core`, `extern crate self as core`, imports named `core`) is checked only inside constructors.rs (lines 64-210), relocations.rs (75-84), iterations.rs (75-89) and predicate_folds.rs (122); other backends that match standard paths by token text rely on whichever subset their author remembered. `Item::ExternCrate` is otherwise ignored, and modules named like std items are loaded without comment. Each new backend must re-derive which names can be shadowed, which is how soundness holes in name resolution get introduced.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":339},"end":{"line":428},"content_hash":"5f612fb245f287bd517cc122d2672f9298a701c992b5e6d9eecf5b3481d13e97"},"suggested_fix":"Centralize prelude/standard-path shadow detection in `Crate::file` (record every module, extern crate rename, import binding, type, trait and generic parameter name per scope) and expose one `Crate::standard_path_unshadowed(path)` query that every backend uses before matching `core::`/`std::`/prelude names by text; reject `extern crate ... as core|std|alloc` globally.","summary":"Standard-name shadow checks are hard-coded lists duplicated across the loader and individual backends instead of one resolver","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543381Z","id":"c5df559dcba99e2e7a0b2f565ce3dbce7d1af16eb67e956c926a86ac8274ace5","body":{"detail":"Obligation validation in project.rs:233-240 only checks that `obligation.function` is a compiled function; `obligation.theorem` is any identifier. lean.rs `generate` emits `theorem «f_correspondence»` inside the project namespace, which mentions `<Ns>.f` in its statement and uses no axioms, so `{\"theorem\":\"Counter.increment_correspondence\",\"function\":\"increment\"}` passes `#provium_check` (Audit.lean) and verified.json reports `invariant_obligations: 1`. methods.rs:1167-1172 has the same gap for the many generated `_refinement`/`_correspondence` theorems. README 'What verification establishes' presents obligations as consumer contracts distinct from the generated correspondence certificates; a copy-paste or placeholder obligation silently turns into a no-op proof. No test in provium/tests covers this.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":233},"end":{"line":240},"content_hash":"8c90d31ad2a30187a766a4b8516e8a6294e4e9a667794af9ab845fa6f0bb1444"},"suggested_fix":"Reject obligations whose theorem is in the generated symbol set (the `symbols` sets already built in project.rs:221-232 and methods.rs:1126-1166, qualified with the namespace), and in Audit.lean optionally require the theorem's declaration module to be `Proofs` or a declared proof module (via `Lean.Environment.getModuleIdxFor?`). Add a regression test.","summary":"An obligation can name a Provium-generated theorem (e.g. `<Ns>.<fn>_correspondence`), so a project certifies an 'invariant obligation' without the consumer proving anything","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543494Z","id":"5eaf889331381c862a728912957cd8fc399b96db787dd828cf5c17c202947d54","body":{"detail":"`output.ancestors().find_map(|p| p.canonicalize().ok())` walks a relative path down to the empty path, which fails to canonicalize, so the current working directory is never consulted. Reproduced with the CLI: from cwd `<scratch>/fx/target/work`, `provium inventory crate --out newout/inv` exits 0 and writes `target/work/newout/inv/inventory.json`. The same check guards `compile`, `verify`, `verify-methods`, `capture-cargo` and the public `verify_project`. README 'Use from your crate' states 'Cargo exclusively owns target/; Provium refuses proof-output paths beneath it.' `verify_suite` is unaffected because it passes an absolute path.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":125},"end":{"line":138},"content_hash":"de19dda9c38bb6740eb4e442da29a9531a6886d5c7cf30316f3150c68314c408"},"suggested_fix":"Absolutize first: `let output = std::path::absolute(output)?` (or `current_dir()?.join(output)`) before the component and ancestor checks, then canonicalize the deepest existing ancestor. Add a test that runs from a cwd inside a `target` directory with a relative nonexistent output.","summary":"prepare_output's target/ refusal is bypassed by a relative output path whose ancestors do not yet exist","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543535Z","id":"2df67b6c2d03c485d59899fe51337c3ebb4e369b4c42f1d87828d0c152663ac2","body":{"detail":"`lean_file(&output, ...)` sets `LEAN_PATH=output`, and project::verify (project.rs:451-474) compiles directly in the output directory, which prepare_output only cleans of verified.json. Any .olean already there is importable: e.g. if the same project directory previously used the methods schema, methods.rs:1448-1463 published `Provium/State.olean`, `Proofs.olean` and consumer proof-module objects such as `Contracts/Storage.olean` into that directory. A scalar Proofs.lean that imports `Contracts.Storage` then loads an object compiled from sources that are neither hashed in this manifest nor rechecked (only Proofs.lean, Generated.lean, etc. are in the artifact list at project.rs:496-511). README promises for method projects that 'Every method verification compiles in a fresh import directory, so omitted libraries cannot resolve to stale artifacts'; the scalar backend does not have that protection.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":413},"end":{"line":433},"content_hash":"b8eb5b66387e8ff06e1d4bad9511115af32182e460527964ce1d9a20a33617ba"},"suggested_fix":"Reuse `methods::proof_modules::Workspace` (or an equivalent fresh `create_dir` build directory) in project::verify and specification::verify, compile only the written sources there, and publish objects to the output directory after the checks pass.","summary":"Scalar `verify` runs Lean with LEAN_PATH set to the reused output directory, so Proofs.lean can import stale .olean files left by earlier runs","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/suite.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543578Z","id":"4eddccc393bb1e97b57bc90b0f2aa9512822ab999daf0d3ee87baa6ef043007d","body":{"detail":"verify_suite removes only `artifacts/provium/verified.json` (line 61) and each currently discovered project's `<out>/verified.json` (line 83). If a project.json is deleted, renamed or moved, its old `artifacts/provium/<old dir>/verified.json` stays next to fresh certificates, and anything that scans the evidence tree (CI artifact upload, coverage `evidence` paths in coverage.rs:47-48) still finds a success marker for a project that no longer verifies. README 'Use from your crate' says the macro writes each project's certificates beneath `artifacts/provium/<project dir>/` and that a suite invalidates previous certificates, which readers will take to cover the whole evidence directory.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":51},"end":{"line":84},"content_hash":"c4c3895356cba9ca294d18ed35c852f0dabf88698f4d91b851272e521c3a6f5f"},"suggested_fix":"Before verifying, walk the output subtree corresponding to the selected proof directory (`output.join(directory.strip_prefix(root))`) and remove every verified.json not in the selected set, or delete that subtree outright, since it is disposable output. Document the behavior.","summary":"verify_suite leaves verified.json of projects no longer in the suite, so stale success certificates survive under artifacts/provium/","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543671Z","id":"f589a16c35a5b07666ab475dd9b8380c158f81981dc4673617fb0d4f8984beae","body":{"detail":"`report` concatenates output from State, Loans, ..., Generated, consumer proof modules, Proofs.lean and Check.lean (lines 1381-1390); line 1401 then compares only the substring count with an expected total. A consumer proof module that runs `#provium_check` while developing proofs, or prints that text, makes verification fail with 'incomplete method axiom audit' even though every check passed. The count also cannot tell which theorem was audited. Soundness currently rests only on Lean's nonzero exit for a failed `#provium_check`. project.rs:475-478 has the same count-only check, although it only reads Check.lean.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":1351},"end":{"line":1411},"content_hash":"bd3e356fe64af5b431b2dd367c6143f69f9919c7d4d79558b379ca1790fb89c1"},"suggested_fix":"Keep Check.lean's output separate, parse each `PROVIUM_VERIFIED <name>; axioms: [...]` line, and require the set of names to equal the generated list of expected theorem names exactly, with no missing or extra names. Ignore output from other files.","summary":"The method axiom-audit completeness check counts `PROVIUM_VERIFIED ` across the output of every compiled Lean file instead of matching the expected theorem names in Check.lean's output","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543711Z","id":"e391384e98805005e3efa71cb911a43f98edb342068ba612b004bc623ac4e09e","body":{"detail":"Line 149 hashes `fs::read(project_path)`, and line 150 calls `read(project_path)`, which reads the file again. verify (lines 479-481) then reads it a third time to find the proofs path and a fourth time for the recheck. If the file changes from A to B and back to A during the run, the run uses B while the certificate records hash(A) and the end-of-run recheck passes. methods.rs:1082-1083 and specification.rs:40-41 already parse the same bytes they hash.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":147},"end":{"line":150},"content_hash":"e6a37e364b6e2ab8815439d79219cd61708d15635d413b1eba665e39204c5d8b"},"suggested_fix":"Read the bytes once, then hash and parse those bytes (split `read` into a `parse(&[u8])` helper). Have verify reuse the parsed Project instead of calling `read` again.","summary":"compile hashes the project file and parses it from two separate reads, so the recorded config_sha256 can describe different bytes than the configuration that was used","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T08:27:25.543745Z","id":"a429360985a7d282cb055040f1c346125bef09f05046d2390638eff615716d47","body":{"detail":"project.rs:518, methods.rs:1466-1467 and specification.rs:123 write verified.json directly. A crash or full disk during the write leaves a truncated verified.json, and tools that treat the file's existence as success will accept it. methods.rs also writes manifest.json and verified.json as two separate non-atomic steps. cargo_capture.rs:474-480 already publishes through a temporary file and `fs::rename`.","kind":"suggestion","span":{"start":{"line":517},"end":{"line":521},"content_hash":"1c65f9e0bbe10c6b33053f90ed237c2f919ea3950cdf82d12c2c792659d978b8"},"suggested_fix":"Add one `publish_certificate(output, bytes)` helper that writes `verified.json.tmp`, calls sync_all, and renames it to verified.json. Use it in all three backends.","summary":"Success certificates are written in place with fs::write instead of an atomic temp-file-plus-rename, unlike captured-build.json","tags":["review","review:2026-10-07-deep","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381516Z","id":"1601c4b9ecc90cd59e0839c1b8d54bda82a3f75d2f8353a0f876b2a453321b72","body":{"detail":"provium/src/project.rs:233-240 only checks that obligation.function names a compiled function; the generated-symbol set built at 221-232 is never checked against obligation.theorem. provium/src/lean.rs:56-57 emits `theorem <fn>_correspondence (env) : Provium.run ... = <fn> env := by rfl` inside the namespace, so its type mentions `<Ns>.<fn>`. project.rs:371-375 writes `#provium_check <theorem> references <Ns>.<fn>` for each obligation, and provium/lean/Provium/Audit.lean only requires a thmInfo whose type mentions the function and whose axioms are in the allowed set, both of which the rfl theorem satisfies. methods.rs:1167-1172 likewise only checks identifier syntax and the method name. No test in provium/tests uses a generated theorem as an obligation (grep for `correspondence\"` finds only queries.rs:57, an unrelated assertion).","kind":"comment","references":"c5df559dcba99e2e7a0b2f565ce3dbce7d1af16eb67e956c926a86ac8274ace5","summary":"confirmed: obligations may name generated <Ns>.<fn>_correspondence theorems and pass the audit","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381549Z","id":"fce60cb46ba163a888762ef3273ad4bb2662a0b33a1bec15c9589cb6d874c21d","body":{"detail":"provium/src/project.rs:131-135 checks the relative path's components and then `ancestors().find_map(canonicalize)`, which ends at the empty path and never consults the cwd. Reproduced with provium/target/debug/provium from cwd <scratch>/fx/target/work: `provium inventory crate --out fresh2/inv` printed 'Inventoried 1 items...', exit=0, and wrote fresh2/inv/inventory.json beneath target/. The same command with an absolute output under target/ failed with 'Cargo exclusively owns target/; use an artifacts/ output directory' (exit 1), as did a relative output whose first component already existed.","kind":"comment","references":"5eaf889331381c862a728912957cd8fc399b96db787dd828cf5c17c202947d54","summary":"confirmed: relative output whose ancestors do not exist bypasses the target/ refusal","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381562Z","id":"10e184e1d63da423e457debd731a5a8155a69f4e9b9b5acbbebf41f2297dcb74","body":{"detail":"provium/src/project.rs:430-431 sets current_dir(output) and LEAN_PATH=output. project::verify (453-474) compiles Semantics, Audit, Generated, Proofs and Check directly in the output directory. prepare_output (125-138) removes only verified.json. methods.rs:1450-1463 publishes Provium/*.olean, Proofs.olean and proof-module .olean objects into the same `out`, and suite.rs:83-90 derives the output only from the project directory, not the backend. The artifact recheck at project.rs:496-514 covers only the written Lean/Rust files, not other importable objects. By contrast, methods uses a fresh proof_modules::Workspace (methods.rs:1344), which README.md:148 describes as a fresh import directory.","kind":"comment","references":"2df67b6c2d03c485d59899fe51337c3ebb4e369b4c42f1d87828d0c152663ac2","summary":"confirmed: scalar verify uses the reused output dir as LEAN_PATH, so stale .olean files are importable","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/suite.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381571Z","id":"67ed9937b941618bcff1fde6487efbff65350f40f9ed6c97dcd9c3edc67e3ec9","body":{"detail":"provium/src/suite.rs:61 removes only artifacts/provium/verified.json, and suite.rs:78-91 calls prepare_output only for each discovered project's `out`. Nothing walks artifacts/provium/ for other verified.json files, so a removed or renamed project's old certificate remains. README.md:62 says 'A suite invalidates all selected previous certificates', which is literally accurate but leaves the stale-certificate case undocumented. coverage.rs:47-48 records an `evidence` path, and its doc comment says accounting does not certify the contents. The concern severity is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"4eddccc393bb1e97b57bc90b0f2aa9512822ab999daf0d3ee87baa6ef043007d","summary":"confirmed: verify_suite only invalidates certificates of currently discovered projects","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381585Z","id":"ba27f7d7d0434b52fa647f55f046fa8fa1f434e0584fe3b01994ca88fbed2517","body":{"detail":"provium/src/methods.rs:1351-1390 appends workspace.check(...) output for every Provium module, Generated.lean, each library, Proofs.lean and Check.lean. Workspace::check (methods/proof_modules.rs:86-88) returns lean_file's combined stdout and stderr. methods.rs:1401-1411 compares only `report.matches(\"PROVIUM_VERIFIED \").count()` against the expected total, so extra PROVIUM_VERIFIED lines from a consumer module or Proofs.lean make the count fail, and the names are never matched. project.rs:474-478 counts only Check.lean's output. The suggestion severity is appropriate, since a failed #provium_check still causes a nonzero Lean exit.","kind":"comment","references":"f589a16c35a5b07666ab475dd9b8380c158f81981dc4673617fb0d4f8984beae","summary":"confirmed: the method audit count spans output from every compiled file, not just Check.lean","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381599Z","id":"90f84322e4534ae2f312a703363425537a3e1a56469a4a4a895f3e05589dc7e0","body":{"detail":"provium/src/project.rs:149 `hash(io(fs::read(project_path))?)` and 150 `read(project_path)?` read the file twice. verify reads it again at 479 (`read(project_path)?`) and 481 (`hash(io(fs::read(project_path))?)`), so an A->B->A change leaves the recorded hash(A) passing the recheck. methods.rs:1082-1083 and specification.rs:40-41 parse the bytes they hash. The suggestion severity is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"e391384e98805005e3efa71cb911a43f98edb342068ba612b004bc623ac4e09e","summary":"confirmed: compile hashes and parses the project file from separate reads","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/project.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:18:12.381606Z","id":"0cff0a17f1b233cccd495dfeadefda428835da706e28615b66972874749a2b60","body":{"detail":"provium/src/project.rs:517-520 calls `io(fs::write(output.join(\"verified.json\"), ...))`. methods.rs:1466-1467 writes manifest.json and then verified.json directly with fs::write. specification.rs:126-127 writes `fs::write(output.join(\"verified.json\"), ...)`. cargo_capture.rs:474-480 instead writes to run/captured-build.json and then calls fs::rename into the output directory. The suggestion severity is appropriate.","kind":"comment","references":"a429360985a7d282cb055040f1c346125bef09f05046d2390638eff615716d47","summary":"confirmed: verified.json is written in place in all three backends, without temp+rename","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/frontend.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:51:36.485524Z","id":"138f97bf1b8d540f2b7c056be142a1d8cffaa68c217fc6cd1d2a5edb551442ca","body":{"detail":"provium/src/frontend.rs:397-399 picks the operand type as `hint(left).or_else(|| hint(right))` for every operator, Shl/Shr included, and hint() at 269-279 does the same for nested binaries. Repro: src.rs `pub fn top_bit(s: u32) -> bool { ((1 << s) >> 31) == 1 }`, project `{\"source\":\"src.rs\",\"namespace\":\"Shift\",\"usize_bits\":64,\"proofs\":null,\"obligations\":[]}`. `cargo run --manifest-path provium/Cargo.toml --locked --offline -- compile project.json --out out` prints `Generated 1 functions`, and Generated.lean has `top_bit_ir := (.binary .eq (.binary .shr (.binary .shl (.uint 32 1) (.var 0)) (.uint 32 31)) (.uint 32 1))`, which is true at s=31 in u32. The same function built with `rustc -C overflow-checks=yes` prints `false` for `top_bit(31)`, because the literal is i32 and the right shift is arithmetic.","kind":"comment","references":"501ebc24ff86130ffefc506897ad8e537d128850eb5543dbd0fd76f76c8adb7a","summary":"confirmed: `1 << s` is typed from the shift count (u32) instead of rustc's i32 fallback, so the generated IR disagrees with native Rust","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/extract.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:51:36.485561Z","id":"e2f12e332a8826521bc3655fb50f459a384809ea7af24e29da18c6171910ffbb","body":{"detail":"Repro: lib.rs has `fn clamp(x: u64) -> u64 { if x > 10 { 10 } else { x } }` and `impl S { pub fn get(&self) -> u64 { clamp(self.a) } pub fn other(&self) -> u64 { self.a + 1 } }`. The project has slices {S::get, name get_value, select [tail], binding self.a->a:u64} and {S::other, name clamp, select [tail], binding self.a->x:u64}. `provium compile` succeeds. out/Source.rs is `fn get_value (a : u64) -> u64 { clamp (a) }` / `fn clamp (x : u64) -> u64 { x + 1 }`, and Generated.lean has `get_value_ir := (.letE (.var 0) (.binary .add (.var 0) (.uint 64 1)))`, which is a+1 and not the crate's clamp. extract.rs:300-312 (Binders visitor) rejects only patterns and macros, not calls. project.rs:202-207 joins the slices into one file, and that file is what Compiler::parse resolves calls against.","kind":"comment","references":"1d4067505bc5499dfabbf9dc8a43348720e0cabb8ef774add40c57d270ff9ffc","summary":"confirmed: a call inside a slice binds to another slice that shares the callee's name, and compile accepts the result","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/extract.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:51:36.485573Z","id":"1e8cbe6a0e6ca2ff34ea4fd183f27561d2ec92ed0328966904a6a01bc31d42eb","body":{"detail":"extract.rs:64-82 counts every `let` whose name appears in parts[1..] and accepts when `matches.len() == parts.len() - 1`. Repro: `pub fn get(&self) -> u64 { let x = self.a + 1; let x = self.a + 1; x }` with select [\"let:x:y\"] compiles (`Generated 1 functions`), and manifest.json records `\"select\": [\"let:x:y\"]` even though the method has no binding named y. Suggestion severity is right because the occurrence-equality check limits the semantic impact.","kind":"comment","references":"37446de3c233e24e49f3cc085322b4658f373feab7b81080883fc5d24468da9e","summary":"confirmed: `let:x:y` is accepted when x is shadowed twice and y does not exist","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:51:36.485583Z","id":"af0a630c0c6b9a347e85f8c0011960c3fc6ef56dd3fd2a1bcedf3d125296c946","body":{"detail":"methods.rs:274 `Item::Use(u) if !test_only(&u.attrs)` and methods.rs:349 `Item::Enum(e) if !test_only(&e.attrs)` never call attrs() or cfg filtering. test_only (methods.rs:145-149) matches only `cfg(test)`, so `#[cfg(feature=..)]` items are recorded. Enum variants are stored without inspection, and ExternCrate/Fn fall into `_ => {}` (methods.rs:488). The Mod, Struct and Impl arms do check attributes (lines 258, 384-405, 428). When self.cfg is None, the configured::item filter (methods.rs:245-254) is skipped. project.rs:166 uses the unconfigured `Crate::load` for scalar_method projects, while methods::verify (methods.rs:1117) uses load_configured. Concern severity is right.","kind":"comment","references":"eb32c17fd2f8822089b28d8b0b830faf520450993d396a7dd1bed5fe4f2a09c2","summary":"confirmed: when no Configuration is given, Crate::file records use/enum items without checking their attributes","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"provium/src/methods.rs","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T09:51:36.485592Z","id":"12e3f3665b4691d7fa3d502dbafe41577a7bf5e9da9da01416f07bac542a5033","body":{"detail":"methods.rs has separate literal lists at the use check (~280-289), enum (339-348), struct (381-389) and impl generics (430-441). The struct generic-parameter checks at methods.rs:685-691 and 733-739 use a shorter list `[\"Option\",\"bool\",\"u8\",\"u16\",\"u32\",\"u64\",\"i32\",\"usize\"]` with no Result/Ok/Err/Some. `core`/`std` shadowing is checked only per backend: constructors.rs:64,187-209, relocations.rs:75-84, iterations.rs:75,89 and predicate_folds.rs:122 (grep '\"core\"'). Crate::file never inspects Item::ExternCrate (falls to `_ => {}`). This is a maintainability and soundness risk, not a concrete failure, so concern is the right severity.","kind":"comment","references":"a9275725a5bf3b92bebc7458a3320287d8ca5c9e463285d1de8b150184e921e5","summary":"confirmed: standard-name shadow lists are hard-coded and inconsistent across the loader and the backends","tags":["verified:confirmed","session:claude-code:a11d54e5-e61a-4ddb-ada4-de40e3db1dad"]}}
